Figure~\ref{fig:var} compares the noise part of the variance of the regression estimate with $\sigma^2L/n$ in 9795 Bernoulli cells. The ratio has median 1.008 (range 0.906 to 1.128). The total variance lies above the noise part when the baseline varies across assignments (median ratio 1.96, up to 419 in the community-baseline runs at $d=80$). The $d$ exponent is $0.55$, not $0.5$: its interval excludes $0.5$. The 42 cells share graphs and draws, so the intervals are Monte Carlo only.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figA2_variance.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{The sampling variance of the regression follows $\sigma^2L/n$.} (A) Noise part of the variance of the regression estimate against $\sigma^2L/n$, one point per Bernoulli cell with a mechanism (a)--(f) ($n=9795$ cells, 107 runs), colour and marker by noise multiple $\sigma$ ($0.25,0.5,1$ planned; $2,4$ extensions); line: identity. Grey: the total variance of the same cells. (B) Fitted exponents of log SD (plain SBM, null column and mechanism (a) at $s=\tfrac12$) on $\log n$, $\log(1+d)$, $\log p(1-p)$ at each planned $\sigma$ (95\% Monte Carlo intervals, 42 cells per $\sigma$); dotted: the predicted exponents $-\tfrac12,+\tfrac12,-\tfrac12$.}
\label{fig:var}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Treated share.}
Figure~\ref{fig:pdep} gives bias, SD and RMSE against $p$ at every degree for mechanisms (a), (c) and the exploratory convex mirror (g). For (a) the bias is within $[-0.005,0.016]$ at every $p$ and $d$. For (c) it runs from $+0.37$, $+0.62$, $+0.67$ at $p=0.1$ to $-0.28$, $-0.39$, $-0.38$ at $p=0.9$ ($d=5,20,80$) and crosses zero at $p=0.397$ [0.393, 0.401] at $d=20$ (step 0.05 over $0.35$--$0.65$ there) and, on the five-point grid, at 0.449 and 0.414 for $d=5$ and $80$. For the convex mirror (g) at $d=20$ it crosses at 0.607 [0.602, 0.612], the mirror image of (c) about $\tfrac12$ within the grid step. SD is smallest at $p=\tfrac12$ in all seven panels that have a $p$ grid ((a) and (c) at $d=5,20,80$; (g) at $d=20$); so is RMSE, except that at $d=20$ the RMSE of (c) is smallest at $p=0.40$, where its bias vanishes, and that of (g) at $0.60$.
